# Which architectures each of robovast's own images is built for.
#
# One source of truth, sourced by container/release_images.sh and read by
# .github/workflows/image.yml, so a local build and a CI build cannot disagree about
# what an image is. Encoding it in both places is how they drift.
#
# The rule is where the image *runs*, not what is convenient to build:
#
#   robovast           developers run this locally (including on arm64 Macs) AND it runs as
#                        a job pod in the cluster -> both architectures. The base is
#                        ros:<distro>-ros-base, a genuine multi-arch index, with the
#                        desktop-full set installed from the pinned snapshot on top; a base
#                        published for amd64 alone would be a single manifest, and an arm64
#                        build would take its amd64 content and label it arm64 -- succeeding
#                        on an amd64 runner and dying with "Invalid ELF image for this
#                        architecture" the moment a real arm64 host execs in it. The same class
#                        of silent mislabelling the CLUSTER_PLATFORM note below warns about, in
#                        the other direction.
#
#                        So a platform listed here is built on a runner of that architecture
#                        (.github/workflows/image.yml maps them) and the merge step asserts the
#                        published index really carries it -- this file cannot promise an
#                        architecture that does not exist.
#   roqsim             both. mujoco and opencv-python-headless publish manylinux aarch64
#                        wheels, and arm64 is tier-1 for Jazzy on Noble, so the debs are there
#                        too. Each architecture builds on a runner native to it and then execs
#                        inside the image it just built -- `uname -m` against the runner's, and
#                        an import of mujoco -- before anything is merged into a tag.
#   controller, sidecar  only ever exist in the cluster, which is always linux/amd64. Nothing
#                        prevents an arm64 controller; it is built amd64-only because that is
#                        where it runs, and widening it should be a change someone makes with a
#                        CI build to prove it.
#
# The architecture every cluster runs, and therefore what `--push` must produce
# regardless of the machine doing the pushing: a `docker build` on an arm64 Mac
# otherwise publishes an image no node can run, and the failure is an obscure
# exec-format error in a pod rather than an error at push time.
CLUSTER_PLATFORM=linux/amd64

PLATFORMS_ROBOVAST=linux/amd64,linux/arm64
PLATFORMS_ROQSIM=linux/amd64,linux/arm64
PLATFORMS_CONTROLLER=linux/amd64
PLATFORMS_SIDECAR=linux/amd64
